{
  "title": "Exploring data with summaries and charts",
  "kicker": "ENCT 202 · Foundation of Data Science · Chapter 4",
  "subtitle": "Before modelling, look: the right chart answers a question in a glance.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "eda",
      "locator": "4.1",
      "title": "Exploratory data analysis",
      "eyebrow": "Look first",
      "body": "<p><em class=\"term\">EDA</em> means summarising and plotting data to find its shape, oddities and relationships before modelling. Start univariate (one variable), then bivariate (pairs), then multivariate.</p>",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "edaq",
          "prompt": "EDA is done…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Only after deployment."
            },
            {
              "text": "Before modelling, to understand the data.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Instead of statistics."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "Too late."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "It guides cleaning and features."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "It complements it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "summ",
      "locator": "4.2",
      "title": "Summary statistics",
      "eyebrow": "Numbers",
      "body": "<p>Centre: mean, median. Spread: standard deviation, IQR. Shape: skewness. For categories: counts and proportions. Group summaries (mean income by region) are the most common first insight — “group by, then aggregate”.</p>",
      "worked": [
        {
          "toc": "Groups",
          "why": "Sales (k): North 10, 12, 14; South 20, 22."
        },
        {
          "toc": "Aggregate",
          "why": "Group means: 12 and 21.",
          "math": "\\bar x_N=12,\\ \\bar x_S=21",
          "hero": true
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "gb",
          "prompt": "“Group by region then mean” produces…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "One average overall."
            },
            {
              "text": "One average per region.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "A sorted table."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "That ignores groups."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "Aggregation over groups."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "Sorting is separate."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "chart",
      "locator": "4.3",
      "title": "Choosing a chart",
      "eyebrow": "Question → picture",
      "body": "<table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Chart</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Distribution of one number</td><td>histogram / box plot</td></tr><tr><td>Compare categories</td><td>bar chart</td></tr><tr><td>Change over time</td><td>line chart</td></tr><tr><td>Relationship of two numbers</td><td>scatter plot</td></tr><tr><td>Part of a whole</td><td>bar (pie only for few parts)</td></tr></tbody></table>",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "chq",
          "prompt": "Monthly rainfall over a year is best shown as…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "A pie chart."
            },
            {
              "text": "A scatter of categories."
            },
            {
              "text": "A line chart.",
              "correct": true
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "Not parts of a whole."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "Loses the order."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "Time on the x-axis."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "corr",
      "locator": "4.4",
      "title": "Relationships",
      "eyebrow": "Pairs",
      "body": "<p>A scatter plot shows direction, strength and curvature; the correlation r summarises only the linear part. For the sample data r = <b>0.9</b> and R² = <b>0.81</b>. A correlation matrix (heat map) scans many pairs at once.</p>",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "rq",
          "prompt": "r near 0 means…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "No linear relationship (there may still be a curve).",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "No relationship of any kind."
            },
            {
              "text": "A strong negative link."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "r measures linear association only."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "A parabola can give r ≈ 0."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "That would be near −1."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "figures": [
        {
          "id": "lab1",
          "caption": "Figure 4.4.1 — scatter and fit",
          "note": "A rising cloud with a fitted line.",
          "script": "var lab = create(\"steplab\", {\"name\":\"sc\",\"engine\":\"regression\",\"points\":\"1,2;2,3;3,5;4,4;5,6\",\"width\":600,\"height\":440});",
          "expect": {
            "sc": {
              "r": "0.9",
              "r2": "0.81"
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mislead",
      "locator": "4.5",
      "title": "Honest charts",
      "eyebrow": "Avoid lying with graphics",
      "body": "<p>Start bar charts at zero; keep scales comparable; label axes and units; do not use 3-D effects; show the sample size. Simpson’s paradox: a trend in every group can reverse when groups are pooled — so check subgroups.</p>",
      "worked": [
        {
          "toc": "Groups",
          "why": "Treatment A cures 81/87 (93 %) of small stones and 192/263 (73 %) of large; B: 234/270 (87 %) and 55/80 (69 %)."
        },
        {
          "toc": "Pooled",
          "why": "A wins in both groups, yet pooled A = 273/350 = 78 % and B = 289/350 = 83 %: the mix of stone sizes reverses the ranking.",
          "hero": true
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "simp",
          "prompt": "Simpson’s paradox warns us to…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Never pool any data."
            },
            {
              "text": "Check subgroups before trusting a pooled trend.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "Trust averages always."
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "Overreaction."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "Confounding can flip the conclusion."
            },
            {
              "title": "It looks right at first.",
              "body": "Exactly the trap."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "story",
      "locator": "4.6",
      "title": "Telling the story",
      "eyebrow": "Communicating",
      "body": "<p>A finding is useful only if the audience acts on it. Lead with the conclusion, show one chart per message, state uncertainty, and note the limits (sample, period, missing data).</p>",
      "questions": [
        {
          "id": "story",
          "prompt": "A good chart title states…",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "The column names only."
            },
            {
              "text": "The software used."
            },
            {
              "text": "The message (“Sales fell 12 % in Q3”).",
              "correct": true
            }
          ],
          "responses": [
            {
              "title": "A tempting idea.",
              "body": "Says nothing."
            },
            {
              "title": "Close, but not it.",
              "body": "Irrelevant."
            },
            {
              "title": "Correct.",
              "body": "Audiences read titles first."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
